From: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: realtek: do not apply the RTL8261C patch to an RTL8261D

The RTL8261C and the RTL8261D share the PHY ID 0x001cc898 and are told
apart by a sub id in extended register 0x801d: 0x00 is the C, 0x81 the D.
Mainline grew that distinction in v7.3, in Realtek's own words: "RTL8261D
is also 10g phy. It's sub_phy_id is 0x81. And it does not need any
firmware." (f0667918d5fc, net: phy: realtek: add support for RTL8261D).

This driver matches both chips on that one ID and pushes the C's patch
script into whatever it finds. On a GL-BE14000 whose 10G port is an
RTL8261D that costs the port: a 1000BASE-T link starts dropping within
half an hour, the drops get denser and the recovery slower until the PHY
stops answering and only removing power brings it back. 100BASE-TX
survives, and so does the GL.iNet stock firmware, whose vendor driver
(rtl8261x-mdio) ships no blob for this PHY at all. Reported on the
OpenWrt forum against r11-r13 and reproduced on the 6.18 snapshot, which
carries the same driver.

Read the sub id and skip the patch for the D. Everything else keeps its
current behaviour, and the detected model is logged so the next report
says which chip it came from.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Andrey Korshunov <razor1702@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/realtek/realtek_main.c
@@ -200,3 +200,6 @@
 /* RTL8261C-CG indirect register addresses */
+#define RTL8261X_SUB_PHY_ID_ADDR		0x801D
+#define RTL8261D_SUB_ID				0x81
+
 #define RTL8261C_INDIRECT_PATCH_VER		0x801E
 #define RTL8261C_INDIRECT_PATCH_SETUP		0x8023
@@ -300,14 +303,35 @@ static int rtl826x_probe(struct phy_device *phydev)
 static int rtl8261c_cg_probe(struct phy_device *phydev)
 {
-	int ret;
+	int ret, sub_id;
 
 	ret = rtl826x_probe(phydev);
 	if (ret < 0)
 		return ret;
 
-	ret = rtl8261c_phy_patch_db_init(phydev);
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return ret;
+	/* The RTL8261C and the RTL8261D share this PHY ID and differ by a
+	 * sub id in extended register 0x801d. The D needs no patch
+	 * firmware at all; applying the C's wrecks its 1000BASE-T links.
+	 */
+	ret = phy_write_mmd(phydev, MDIO_MMD_VEND2,
+		            RTL8261C_VND2_INDIRECT_ADDR,
+		            RTL8261X_SUB_PHY_ID_ADDR);
+	if (ret < 0)
+		return ret;
+
+	sub_id = phy_read_mmd(phydev, MDIO_MMD_VEND2,
+		              RTL8261C_VND2_INDIRECT_DATA);
+	if (sub_id < 0)
+		return sub_id;
+
+	sub_id = (sub_id >> 8) & 0xff;
+	phydev_info(phydev, "RTL8261%c detected (sub_id 0x%02x)\n",
+		    sub_id == RTL8261D_SUB_ID ? 'D' : 'C', sub_id);
+
+	if (sub_id != RTL8261D_SUB_ID) {
+		ret = rtl8261c_phy_patch_db_init(phydev);
+		if (ret < 0)
+			return ret;
+	}
 
 	/* Datasheet requires bit 0 = 1 for the TSSR block to be valid. */
 	phy_modify_mmd(phydev, MDIO_MMD_VEND2, RTL8261C_VND2_THERMAL_SENSOR_CTRL,
